///1MSCOBOL
Microsoft COBOL-80: compiling and running programs under CP/M-80.
This section describes the program workflow, not the COBOL language.
Compiler filenames and prompts vary by release. Follow the manual for
the exact Microsoft COBOL-80 package installed on your disks.

///2DRIVES
The configured drives for this system are:

  A: CP/M system disk; read-only
  B: language.dsk; compiler and language files
  C: Utility.dsk; utility files
  D: not configured
  E: work1.dsk; writable source and output disk
  F: work2.dsk; writable second work disk

Use E: for source, object, listing, and program files. Start the
compiler from B: by using its drive-qualified command name. A drive
prefix selects a disk; CP/M does not use folders. Names use the 8.3
form, such as HELLO.CBL, HELLO.REL, and HELLO.COM.

///2CHECKING
At the CP/M prompt, list the work and compiler disks:

  A>DIR E:
  A>DIR B:

Confirm the compiler and source files are present. Do not assume that
the compiler is installed just because B: is named language.dsk.
Never save source or output on A:, because that disk is read-only.
Use a disposable work disk while learning.

///2COMPILING
Select E: so compiler output defaults to the writable work disk:

  A>E:
  E>

Check the source filename:

  E>DIR HELLO.*

If the source is HELLO.CBL and the compiler program is COBOL.COM,
a common command-line form is:

  E>B:COBOL E:HELLO.CBL

This command is only an example. The installed release may use a
different compiler filename, source suffix, command syntax, or
interactive prompts. Use the exact syntax in its manual. Give the
source with its E: drive prefix when requested.

Read the compiler messages. Correct errors, save the source on E:,
and compile again. Check which files were created:

  E>DIR HELLO.*

A .REL file is normally a relocatable object, not a runnable program.
If the compiler creates a .COM file directly, follow its manual to
run it. Otherwise, use the linker required by that COBOL release.

///2LINKING
The system disk provides the CP/M LINK utility. If the compiler
manual says to use it, select E: and invoke LINK from A::

  E>A:LINK E:HELLO

This is a typical DRI LINK-style example. The linker may instead
prompt for filenames or require compiler runtime libraries, options,
or a different linker. Follow the installed compiler manual. Ensure
that the linker can find HELLO.REL and its required libraries, and
that the .COM output is written to E:.

Check the result:

  E>DIR HELLO.*

Do not run the program if linking reported unresolved modules or did
not create the expected command file.

///2RUNNING
Select E: and start the executable by name, without the .COM suffix:

  A>E:
  E>HELLO

The program runs as a CP/M transient command. Keep its data files on
E: unless the program's documentation specifies another drive. If
the program reports a missing module or runtime file, use matching
libraries from the same compiler release.

///2PROBLEMS
Bad command or command file not found:
Check DIR B: and use the compiler's actual filename.

Source file not found:
Check DIR E: and enter the exact source name with its extension.

Compile succeeded but no .COM exists:
The compiler may have produced only a .REL object. Link it if the
compiler manual requires a separate link step.

Disk is read-only or a write failed:
Do not write to A:. Select E: and confirm it is a writable disk.

Missing module or library:
Use runtime and library files for the same compiler release.

